### Method Explanation
The problem asks for the sum of $f(P)$ across all permutations of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$. 
The contribution of each element $k \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$ when placed in a permutation depends on the sum of the digit-lengths of all elements positioned after it. 
Specifically, the value contributed by $k$ is $k \times 10^{\text{sum of lengths of elements after } k}$.
If we fix the subset $S$ of elements appearing after $k$, there are $|S|! (N - 1 - |S|)!$ valid arrangements for the remaining elements.

We can express the total answer using the Eulerian Beta integral. The weight $|S|! (N - 1 - |S|)!$ can be elegantly rewritten as $N! \int_0^1 t^{|S|} (1-t)^{N-1-|S|} dt$. 
Summing this over all elements $k$ and subsets $S$ yields:
$$ \text{Total} = N! \int_0^1 \sum_{k=1}^N k \prod_{x \neq k} (t \cdot 10^{\text{len}(x)} + 1 - t) dt $$

Let $v_L = 10^L - 1$. The term $t \cdot 10^{\text{len}(x)} + 1 - t$ simplifies to $1 + v_L t$. 
Let $P(t) = \prod_{L=1}^6 (1 + v_L t)^{c_L}$, where $c_L$ is the number of integers in $1 \dots N$ with length $L$.
The integrand simplifies to a polynomial $Q(t) = \sum_{L=1}^6 S_L \frac{P(t)}{1 + v_L t}$ where $S_L$ is the sum of integers in $1 \dots N$ with length $L$.
Because $Q(t)$ is a polynomial of degree $N-1$, $\int_0^1 Q(t) dt$ corresponds to taking the coefficients $q_k$ of $t^k$ in $Q(t)$ and evaluating $\sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \frac{q_k}{k+1}$.

Instead of computing the polynomial $Q(t)$ by naive expansion (which would take $O(N^2)$), we take advantage of linear recurrences created by logarithmic differentiation. We can deduce $p_k$ (the coefficients of $P(t)$) in $O(N \cdot \text{max\_len})$ time because $P'(t) \prod (1 + v_L t) = P(t) \sum c_L v_L \prod_{M \neq L} (1 + v_M t)$. A similar iterative matching allows us to find $q_k$ sequentially in $O(N \cdot \text{max\_len})$.

